2026, day 19

Good morning from Asia. Another “quiet day”.

‘Bessent says Europe is too weak to guarantee Greenland’s security’ (FT) - “US refuses to back down in the face of outrage among its allies over plan to acquire Danish territory”;

‘German industry lashes out at Trump's 'ludicrous' demands’ (Reuters);

‘‘It’s about trust’: Donald Trump’s fresh tariff threats push Europe to harden its stance’ (FT);

‘EU Leaders to Meet on US Tariff Threat, Potential Retaliation’ (Bloomberg);

‘Hit back at Trump? Europe mulls unthinkable options as Greenland threats ramp up tensions’ (Politico);

‘Europe already ‘at war’ with Russia, says central banker’ (FT) - “Latvijas Banka governor and ECB policymaker urges policymakers to prepare financial system for direct military attack”;

‘In Trump’s Drive for Greenland, NATO Is the First Casualty’ (WSJ);

‘Trump's latest Western Hemisphere fixation: Canada’ (NBC) - “The president has intensified his criticism of Canada in private conversations with aides in recent weeks over what he sees as the country's vulnerability to U.S. adversaries in the Arctic”;

‘Iran doctors put death toll in suppressed uprising at over 16,500’ (ToI);

‘Anti-regime activists hack Iran's national broadcaster, transmit Pahlavi's calls to protest’ (JPost);

‘'Over 90% of Iranians hate the regime,' INSS’s Beni Sabti reveals from leaked Iran survey’ (JPost);

‘'All options on the table': US boosts presence in Middle East as Iran strike remains possibility’ (JPost);

‘World leaders show caution on Trump’s broader ‘Board of Peace’ amid fears for UN’ (SCMP);

‘Blair Distances Himself From Trump’s $1 Billion Peace Board Fee’ (Bloomberg);

‘‘Survival of the Richest’: Trump’s Chaos Sets Off Rush to Davos’ (Bloomberg) - “A record crowd pursues the US president to the annual conclave as he shakes up trade and alliances”;

In geoeconomics, ‘Europe threatens to strike back against Trump over Greenland tariffs’ (Telegraph) - “Brussels readies €93bn in retaliatory levies against US exports as PM pledges further talks with president”;

‘EU trade ‘bazooka’ back on the table, as Greenland tariff threat blows up US deal’ (Euractiv);

‘Trump plunges Britain’s carmakers back into crisis’ (Telegraph);

‘Beijing pours cash into Belt and Road financing in global resources grab’ (FT);

‘U.S. Blockade Shuts Off China, Cuba From Venezuelan Oil’ (WSJ) - “Only U.S.-bound tankers of crude have left the country since the arrest of Nicolás Maduro, a report says”;

‘Israel, U.S. Sign Joint Statement on AI Cooperation in Jerusalem, Advancing Strategic Partnership’ (Vinnews);

‘Philippines Makes Significant Natural Gas Discovery, Marcos Says’ (Bloomberg);

‘Carney’s China embrace widens gap with U.S. in Trump’s tariff era’ (Bloomberg);

In the economy, ‘Why the Tech World Thinks the American Dream Is Dying’ (WSJ) - “Silicon Valley fears this is the last chance to amass generational wealth before AI makes money worthless”;

‘Economists Are Studying the Slowing Job Market—and Feeling It Themselves’ (WSJ) - “Newly minted Ph.D.s tend to work for universities, government agencies and big white-collar companies. It’s not a great hiring time for any of them”;

‘Indonesia turns to internships to quell youth unemployment’ (Nikkei Asia) - “Government subsidizes work experience, but unclear if it will lead to full-time jobs”;

In politics, ‘Pentagon Places 1,500 Soldiers on Alert for Possible Minnesota’ (WSJ) - “Deployment, Officials Say Move comes as Trump has threatened to invoke Insurrection Act in response to widespread protests”;

‘Senior Tory MP defects to Reform’ (Telegraph);

‘Newspoll: One Nation ahead of Coalition, PM takes Bondi bruising’ (Australian);

‘US considering asylum for British Jews’ (Telegraph) - “Rising anti-Semitism means UK ‘no longer a safe place,’ Donald Trump’s personal lawyer tells The Telegraph”;

In markets, ‘Gold Rises to Record High After Trump’s Greenland Tariff Threats’ (Bloomberg)
